1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,345
A new student walks into class
and sits next to their friend.

2
00:00:04,345 --> 00:00:06,902
A magnet pulls a pin to its two ends.

3
00:00:06,902 --> 00:00:09,838
Smoke from a chimney
leaves black soot behind.

4
00:00:10,238 --> 00:00:12,249
Seats are not chosen at random.

5
00:00:12,249 --> 00:00:14,167
And some soot hides a danger.

6
00:00:14,617 --> 00:00:16,826
Aarav sees it at his study desk.

7
00:00:16,826 --> 00:00:20,534
Toluene and nitrobenzene sit
side by side in his lab book.

8
00:00:20,884 --> 00:00:25,039
Add a nitro group to toluene, and
it goes next door or opposite.

9
00:00:25,039 --> 00:00:28,053
Add one to nitrobenzene,
and it skips a seat.

10
00:00:28,553 --> 00:00:31,633
Why does the group already
on the ring choose the seat?

11
00:00:31,633 --> 00:00:34,191
By the end, you will
predict it every time.

12
00:00:34,841 --> 00:00:36,816
So what is directive influence?

13
00:00:37,166 --> 00:00:41,777
When benzene already carries a group, a
new group does not land just anywhere.

14
00:00:42,127 --> 00:00:44,728
The seat next to the
group is called ortho.

15
00:00:44,728 --> 00:00:46,231
Two seats away is meta.

16
00:00:46,231 --> 00:00:47,726
Straight across is para.

17
00:00:48,076 --> 00:00:51,347
The group already there decides
which seats get the new group.

18
00:00:51,347 --> 00:00:53,436
This is called its directive influence.

19
00:00:53,836 --> 00:00:55,525
It also decides the speed.

20
00:00:55,525 --> 00:00:57,917
Some groups make the ring react faster.

21
00:00:57,917 --> 00:00:59,223
They are activating.

22
00:00:59,623 --> 00:01:01,452
Others make it react slower.

23
00:01:01,452 --> 00:01:02,887
They are deactivating.

24
00:01:03,537 --> 00:01:07,892
Let's watch phenol, with an OH
group, on our molecule bench.

25
00:01:08,242 --> 00:01:09,798
Oxygen has lone pairs.

26
00:01:09,798 --> 00:01:13,175
Think of a friend sharing
toys with the whole class.

27
00:01:13,575 --> 00:01:15,285
One lone pair slides into the ring.

28
00:01:15,285 --> 00:01:16,190
Watch the arrows.

29
00:01:16,190 --> 00:01:17,709
The ring's electrons shift along.

30
00:01:18,109 --> 00:01:20,892
A negative charge appears
at an ortho carbon.

31
00:01:20,892 --> 00:01:24,739
Shift again, and it moves to
the other ortho, then to para.

32
00:01:25,139 --> 00:01:27,175
So ortho and para get extra electrons.

33
00:01:27,175 --> 00:01:28,561
The meta seats get none.

34
00:01:28,961 --> 00:01:32,205
An electrophile loves electrons,
so it lands at ortho and para.

35
00:01:32,205 --> 00:01:34,849
The extra electrons also
make the ring react faster.

36
00:01:35,249 --> 00:01:38,547
OH is an ortho para director,
and it activates the ring.

37
00:01:39,197 --> 00:01:40,170
Now nitrobenzene.

38
00:01:40,170 --> 00:01:42,716
The nitro group pulls
electrons out of the ring.

39
00:01:43,116 --> 00:01:44,781
Its arrows go the other way.

40
00:01:44,781 --> 00:01:47,075
Positive charges appear at ortho and para.

41
00:01:47,475 --> 00:01:49,672
Those seats become poor in electrons.

42
00:01:49,672 --> 00:01:52,842
Meta is less poor, so the
electrophile goes to meta.

43
00:01:53,242 --> 00:01:56,880
And with electrons pulled away,
the whole ring reacts more slowly.

44
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:59,960
Nitro is a meta director,
and it deactivates the ring.

45
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:01,435
Pause and think.

46
00:02:01,435 --> 00:02:04,179
Methyl gives electrons,
and CHO pulls them.

47
00:02:04,179 --> 00:02:06,391
Which one sends a new group to meta?

48
00:02:09,591 --> 00:02:10,491
CHO!

49
00:02:10,182 --> 00:02:12,215
It pulls electrons away, like nitro.

50
00:02:12,215 --> 00:02:15,883
Methyl gives electrons, so it sends
the new group to ortho and para.

51
00:02:16,533 --> 00:02:19,153
Let's sort the groups, one link at a time.

52
00:02:19,503 --> 00:02:20,403
Link one.

53
00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:25,953
Ortho para directors that activate:
OH, NH two, NHR, NHCOCH three,

54
00:02:25,953 --> 00:02:28,270
OCH three, methyl and ethyl.

55
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:29,920
Link two.

56
00:02:29,688 --> 00:02:32,953
They give electrons to the ring,
by resonance or by pushing.

57
00:02:33,703 --> 00:02:34,686
Link three.

58
00:02:34,686 --> 00:02:40,341
Meta directors that deactivate:
nitro, CN, CHO, COR, COOH, CO

59
00:02:40,341 --> 00:02:42,126
OR and SO three H.

60
00:02:42,876 --> 00:02:43,776
Link four.

61
00:02:43,423 --> 00:02:44,977
They pull electrons out of the ring.

62
00:02:45,727 --> 00:02:46,627
Link five.

63
00:02:46,408 --> 00:02:47,857
Halogens are the odd ones.

64
00:02:47,857 --> 00:02:50,787
They pull electrons through
the bond, so they deactivate.

65
00:02:50,787 --> 00:02:53,246
But their lone pairs
still feed ortho and para.

66
00:02:53,996 --> 00:02:54,929
Remember this.

67
00:02:54,929 --> 00:02:56,952
Givers send it to ortho and para.

68
00:02:56,952 --> 00:02:58,478
Pullers send it to meta.

69
00:02:59,178 --> 00:03:01,140
Now, one worked example.

70
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:04,055
Nitration of toluene and of nitrobenzene.

71
00:03:04,355 --> 00:03:05,255
Step one.

72
00:03:05,059 --> 00:03:06,692
Toluene has a methyl group.

73
00:03:06,692 --> 00:03:08,049
Methyl gives electrons.

74
00:03:08,799 --> 00:03:09,699
Step two.

75
00:03:09,602 --> 00:03:12,431
So the nitro group goes to ortho and para.

76
00:03:12,431 --> 00:03:16,737
We get ortho nitrotoluene and para
nitrotoluene, faster than benzene.

77
00:03:17,487 --> 00:03:18,387
Step three.

78
00:03:18,347 --> 00:03:20,740
Nitrobenzene already has a nitro group.

79
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:22,171
Nitro pulls electrons.

80
00:03:22,921 --> 00:03:23,821
Step four.

81
00:03:23,615 --> 00:03:25,754
So the second nitro group goes to meta.

82
00:03:25,754 --> 00:03:27,222
We get meta dinitrobenzene.

83
00:03:27,972 --> 00:03:32,294
And it needs harsher conditions,
because the ring has been slowed down.

84
00:03:32,944 --> 00:03:34,595
Now the danger in the soot.

85
00:03:34,995 --> 00:03:36,600
Benzene itself is toxic.

86
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:40,844
And hydrocarbons with more than two
benzene rings fused together can cause

87
00:03:40,844 --> 00:03:41,744
cancer.

88
00:03:41,470 --> 00:03:43,170
We call them carcinogenic.

89
00:03:43,570 --> 00:03:46,459
They form when things
burn without enough air.

90
00:03:46,459 --> 00:03:48,356
Tobacco, coal and petroleum.

91
00:03:48,756 --> 00:03:50,889
Examples are one two benzanthracene,
three methylcholanthrene and one two

92
00:03:50,889 --> 00:03:51,789
benzpyrene.

93
00:03:51,705 --> 00:03:56,747
Inside the body, they react step by
step, damage DNA, and can cause cancer.

94
00:03:56,747 --> 00:03:59,805
That is one more reason
to stay away from smoke.

95
00:04:00,455 --> 00:04:01,916
Now, the exam trap.

96
00:04:02,266 --> 00:04:05,989
Students say chlorine deactivates the
ring, so it must be a meta director.

97
00:04:06,389 --> 00:04:07,289
Wrong.

98
00:04:06,868 --> 00:04:10,412
Chlorine deactivates by pulling through
the bond, but its lone pairs feed

99
00:04:10,412 --> 00:04:11,337
ortho and para.

100
00:04:11,337 --> 00:04:12,953
It is an ortho para director.

101
00:04:13,603 --> 00:04:14,910
Where is this asked?

102
00:04:14,910 --> 00:04:18,690
Questions on predicting the main
product, and on which groups activate

103
00:04:18,690 --> 00:04:19,629
or deactivate.

104
00:04:20,229 --> 00:04:21,302
Exam level now.

105
00:04:21,302 --> 00:04:22,874
Chlorobenzene is nitrated.

106
00:04:22,874 --> 00:04:27,137
Where does the nitro group mainly go,
and is the reaction faster or slower

107
00:04:27,137 --> 00:04:28,301
than for benzene?

108
00:04:28,301 --> 00:04:29,419
Pause and solve.

109
00:04:34,819 --> 00:04:36,074
The answer is D.

110
00:04:36,074 --> 00:04:38,019
Ortho and para, and slower.

111
00:04:38,519 --> 00:04:40,653
Chlorine's lone pairs feed ortho and para.

112
00:04:40,653 --> 00:04:42,309
So the nitro group goes there.

113
00:04:42,809 --> 00:04:46,427
But chlorine pulls electrons through
the bond, so the ring is slower than

114
00:04:46,427 --> 00:04:47,327
benzene.

115
00:04:47,487 --> 00:04:49,634
A and B say meta, the trap.

116
00:04:49,634 --> 00:04:52,339
C says faster, which forgets the pull.

117
00:04:52,938 --> 00:04:54,479
Here is your recap.

118
00:04:54,479 --> 00:04:56,061
Screenshot this card.

119
00:04:59,261 --> 00:05:01,447
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

120
00:05:01,847 --> 00:05:02,747
One.

121
00:05:02,270 --> 00:05:04,283
Why is OH an ortho para director?

122
00:05:05,083 --> 00:05:05,983
Two.

123
00:05:05,550 --> 00:05:08,758
Where does a new group go
in nitrobenzene, and why?

124
00:05:09,558 --> 00:05:10,458
Three.

125
00:05:10,041 --> 00:05:12,619
How are carcinogenic
polynuclear hydrocarbons formed?

126
00:05:13,519 --> 00:05:15,876
That completes Class eleven chemistry!

127
00:05:15,876 --> 00:05:20,570
Next, in Class twelve: why does salty
water boil at a higher temperature?

128
00:05:20,570 --> 00:05:22,302
We find out in solutions.
